# DESCRIPTION
# Using study habits of college students, perform the initial steps of a two-sample t-test.
# ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('WeBWorK')
## DBchapter('Nursing-Med Calc')
## DBsection('Problem')
## KEYWORDS('null hypothesis', 'alternative hypothesis', 'two-sample test')
## Author('Catherine Buell')
## Institution('Fitchburg State')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", 'PGML.pl', "parserPopUp.pl", "PGcourse.pl");

Context("Numeric")->flags->set(
    tolerance => 0.02,
    tolType   => "absolute",
);

@opts = ('\(<\)', '\(>\)', '\(=\)', '\(\neq\)');
$dd1  = DropDown([@opts], 2, useHTMLSelect => 0);
$dd2  = DropDown([@opts], 1, useHTMLSelect => 0);

$n1 = random(50, 70);
$n2 = random(20, 40);

do {
    $gpa1 = random(2.8, 3.5, 0.01);
    $gpa2 = random(2.8, 3.5, 0.01);
} until $gpa1 - $gpa2 > 0.05;

$sig_level = list_random(1, 2, 5, 10);
$pval      = random(1, 99) * list_random(0.001, 0.0001, 0.00001);

$dd3 = DropDown(
    [
        "We will reject the null hypothesis.",
        "We fail to reject the null hypothesis."
    ],
    $pval < $sig_level / 100 ? 0 : 1
);

BEGIN_PGML

Researchers surveyed [$n1+$n2] students at Saint Lawrence University. They asked
students whether or not they have ever pulled an all-nighter (stayed up all
night studying), and they wanted to know if students who have never pulled
an all-nighter have better grades on average. Researchers found that students
who claimed to have never pulled an all-nighter (group 1, [$n1] students) had 
an average GPA (grade point average) of [$gpa1], compared to [$gpa2] for students 
who claimed to have pulled an all-nighter (group 2, [$n2] students). They do a 
hypothesis test at a [$sig_level]% level of significance.

a) The null hypothesis would be: [`H_0`]: [`\mu_{1}`] [_]{$dd1} [`\mu_{2}`]

b) The alternative hypothesis would be: [`H_a`]:  [`\mu_{1}`] [_]{$dd2}   [`\mu_{2}`]

c) What is [`n_1`]? [_]{$n1}

d) What is [`n_2`]? [_]{$n2}

e) What is [`\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2`] (to two decimal places)? [_]{Real($gpa1-$gpa2) }

f) If the p-value is [$pval], what should be our conclusion? [_]{$dd3}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) The null hypothesis is the neutral relationship and that is [`\mu_1=\mu_2`].

b) Since the researchers want to know if student who have never pulled an all-nighter
have better grades than since [`\mu_1`] is the mean GPA of these students and 
[`\mu_2`] is the mean GPA of student who have.  Then the alternative hypothesis is
 [`\mu_1 > \mu_2`].

c) [`n_1`] is the number of sample in group 1 or [`[$n1]`].

d) [`n_2`] is the number of sample in group 2 or [`[$n2]`].

e) The problem states that [`\bar{x}_1=[$gpa1]`] and [`\bar{x}_2 = [$gpa2]`] so 
    [`\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2 = [$gpa1-$gpa2] `].
f) Since the [`p`]-value is [@ $pval < $sig_level/100 ? 'smaller' : 'greater'@] than
    the level of significant of [`\alpha = [$sig_level/100]`], converted to a 
    fraction, the reserchers should 
    *[@ $pval < $sig_level/100 ? '' : 'not '@]reject the null hypothesis*.

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
